/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/fortinet-fortigate.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("fortinet-fortigate", [["fg1",0,1,"A new FortiGate appliance is unboxed and an admin cables a laptop to the MGMT port. Which address should they browse to for the first GUI login?",["https://192.168.1.99","https://192.168.100.99:10443","https://10.0.0.1","https://172.16.1.1"],0,"Hardware FortiGates ship with 192.168.1.99 on the management (or internal/port1) interface. The other addresses are common defaults on other vendors' gear, not FortiGate.","Initial configuration"],["fg2",0,1,"What happens the first time an admin logs in to a factory-default FortiGate appliance?",["They log in as admin with the password fortinet and are then asked to register","They log in as root with the unit's serial number","They must first register the unit in FortiCloud","They log in as admin with a blank password and must set one"],3,"The default account is admin with an empty password, and FortiOS forces a new password at first login. There is no root account, and FortiCloud registration is optional for local login.","Initial configuration"],["fg3",0,1,"Security policy says administrators may only log in to the FortiGate from the 10.10.99.0/24 management subnet. Which administrator setting enforces this most directly?",["A stricter admin password policy","A custom administrator profile limited to the management VDOM","Trusted hosts on each administrator account","Two-factor authentication for admins"],2,"Trusted hosts limit the source addresses an admin account can log in from. Profiles control what an admin may change, not where they log in from, and MFA or password rules do not restrict source networks.","Initial configuration"],["fg4",0,1,"An auditor needs to view logs and reports on the FortiGate but must not change any settings. What should the admin create?",["A super_admin account restricted with trusted hosts on the audit subnet","An admin profile with read-only log and report access","A local firewall user in a user group","A new VDOM holding only log settings"],1,"Admin profiles set read or read-write access per area, so a read-only profile for Log & Report fits. super_admin can change everything, and firewall users cannot log in to administration at all.","Initial configuration"],["fg5",0,1,"In an FGCP cluster with HA override disabled (the default), which criterion is compared first when electing the primary?",["Device priority","Number of connected monitored interfaces","HA uptime","The serial number, highest value winning"],1,"With override disabled, FGCP compares monitored interfaces, then HA uptime, then priority, then serial number. Priority is only checked before uptime when override is enabled.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster"],["fg6",0,1,"An admin raises the HA priority of the secondary FortiGate to 250, but it stays secondary. Override is disabled and both units have all monitored interfaces up. Why?",["HA uptime is compared before priority while override is disabled","Priority only applies after the cluster is rebooted twice","A priority above 200 is ignored by FGCP","The secondary's serial number is lower than the primary's"],0,"With override disabled, the unit with the higher HA uptime (beyond the uptime margin) stays primary regardless of priority. Enabling override, or resetting uptime, makes priority take effect.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster"],["fg7",0,1,"After an HA failover, users complain that long file transfers and SSH sessions dropped. What should the admin enable so TCP sessions survive the next failover?",["HA override on both cluster members","A second dedicated heartbeat interface","Uninterruptible firmware upgrade","Session pickup in the HA settings"],3,"Session pickup synchronizes the session table so the new primary can continue existing TCP sessions; it is disabled by default. Override and extra heartbeat links affect election and resilience, not session continuity.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster"],["fg8",0,1,"Two HA members show as out of sync in the GUI. Which command compares configuration checksums across the cluster members?",["get system performance status","diagnose sys session list","diagnose sys ha checksum cluster","diagnose debug flow trace start 10"],2,"diagnose sys ha checksum cluster shows the checksum of each configuration area on every member so you can find what differs. The others show resources, sessions and packet flow.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster"],["fg9",0,1,"Which settings must match for two FortiGates to form an FGCP cluster?",["Hostname, management IP address and the admin password","Serial number prefix and license expiry dates","Model, firmware, HA group ID, group name and password","Interface IP addresses on every port"],2,"FGCP requires the same hardware model and firmware and matching HA group ID, group name and password. Hostnames and management IPs are unique per member.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster"],["fg10",0,1,"How does FGCP active-active mode differ from active-passive mode?",["Every member owns its own set of interface IP addresses","The primary shares security-profile inspection sessions with secondaries","The secondaries route traffic with no connection to the primary","Session synchronization is turned off by design"],1,"In active-active, the primary still receives traffic but distributes sessions that need inspection to the secondaries. In active-passive only the primary processes traffic. The cluster shares virtual IP and MAC addresses in both modes.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster"],["fg11",0,1,"A branch FortiGate must join the headquarters Security Fabric, where HQ is the root. What is the correct approach?",["Enable Fabric on the branch, point it at the root and authorize it there","Import the root's configuration backup onto the branch","Build an IPsec tunnel and enable fabric-sync on it","Register both units under the same FortiCare account only"],0,"A downstream FortiGate enables the Security Fabric connection, uses the upstream (root) IP, and the root authorizes it by serial number. Sharing a FortiCare account or a tunnel does not by itself create the Fabric.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric"],["fg12",0,1,"What must the root FortiGate have before the Security Fabric can be set up?",["A FortiManager managing every downstream unit","At least one FortiSwitch connected over FortiLink","A dedicated HA heartbeat link to each downstream unit","Logging to FortiAnalyzer or a cloud logging service"],3,"The Fabric root needs FortiAnalyzer or cloud logging (such as FortiGate Cloud) so Fabric logs and views can be built. FortiManager and FortiSwitch are optional Fabric devices.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric"],["fg13",0,1,"The security team wants an email every time any administrator changes the FortiGate configuration. What is the simplest way to do this?",["A syslog server with a filter that forwards matching events to a shared mailbox","An event log report scheduled once per day","A FortiGuard outbreak alert subscription","An automation stitch with a configuration-change trigger and an email action"],3,"Automation stitches pair a trigger (such as a configuration change) with actions (such as email). A daily report is not immediate, and syslog forwarding needs extra tools.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric"],["fg14",0,1,"What does the Security Rating feature provide?",["A FortiGuard reputation score for each external website","Live bandwidth ratings for each WAN link","Best-practice checks across Fabric devices with a score and fixes","A per-user risk score based on failed logins"],2,"Security Rating runs checks against Fortinet best practices on Fabric devices and shows a score with recommendations. It does not rate websites or WAN links.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric"],["fg15",0,1,"A FortiGate enters memory conserve mode during a traffic spike. Which global setting decides whether sessions needing proxy-based antivirus pass uninspected or are blocked?",["ips fail-open","av-failopen","tcp-halfopen-timer","admin-lockout-threshold"],1,"The av-failopen setting under system global controls what proxy inspection does in conserve mode. IPS fail-open controls the IPS engine, and the others are timers or lockout settings.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems"],["fg16",0,1,"Which command gives a quick summary of CPU use, memory use, session counts and uptime on a FortiGate?",["get system performance status","get system status","get router info routing-table all","diagnose sys ha status"],0,"get system performance status summarizes CPU, memory, sessions and uptime. get system status shows firmware, serial and mode, not live resources.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems"],["fg17",0,1,"A debug flow for a failing connection shows \"Denied by forward policy check (policy 0)\". What does this tell the admin?",["No policy matched, so the implicit deny dropped the traffic","Policy ID 0 is a user policy waiting for authentication","The route lookup failed before any firewall policy was checked","An IPS sensor on the first policy blocked the session"],0,"Policy 0 is the implicit deny at the bottom of the policy list, so no configured policy matched. A routing failure or IPS block shows a different debug message.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems"],["fg18",0,1,"An admin runs diagnose debug flow filter addr 10.1.1.5 and diagnose debug flow trace start 20, but no output appears while the host sends traffic. What was missed?",["diagnose debug reset","execute ping-options source 10.1.1.5","diagnose sys session clear","diagnose debug enable"],3,"Debug output is only printed to the console after diagnose debug enable. Resetting debug clears filters, and clearing sessions would disrupt users without showing anything.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems"],["fg19",0,1,"A policy logs only security events, but the analyst needs a record of every accepted session through it. What should be changed?",["Enable log violation traffic on policy 0","Raise the event log severity to debug","Set Log allowed traffic to All sessions","Enable local disk logging for event logs"],2,"All sessions logs every accepted session; Security events only logs sessions a security profile acted on. Implicit-deny logging records denied traffic, not allowed sessions.","Deployment and system configuration: logging"],["fg20",0,1,"A small FortiGate without a disk logs to memory. After a reboot for a firmware upgrade, yesterday's logs are gone. What should the admin do?",["Increase the memory log size to the maximum the model allows","Send logs to FortiAnalyzer, FortiGate Cloud or syslog","Lower the log severity from information to warning","Enable log compression on the memory buffer"],1,"Memory logs are small and lost on reboot, so keeping history needs a remote log destination. A bigger or compressed memory buffer is still cleared on restart.","Deployment and system configuration: logging"],["fg21",0,1,"What is a virtual domain (VDOM) on a FortiGate?",["A DNS zone that the FortiGate hosts and answers for internal clients","A separate instance with its own policies, routes and settings","A group of admins who share one administrator profile","A VLAN that carries HA heartbeat traffic"],1,"Each VDOM acts like an independent FortiGate with its own interfaces, routing and policies. DNS zones, admin groups and VLANs are unrelated features.","Initial configuration"],["fg22",0,1,"A FortiGate runs an old 7.0 build and must be moved to FortiOS 7.6. What should the admin do first?",["Back up the config and follow the published upgrade path","Install the 7.6 image directly and reboot twice","Factory reset, then restore the 7.0 configuration file onto 7.6","Upgrade the secondary HA member only and wait"],0,"Fortinet publishes an upgrade path that steps through intermediate builds so the configuration converts cleanly, and a backup allows rollback. Skipping steps can lose settings.","Initial configuration"],["fg23",0,2,"How does a FortiGate choose which firewall policy applies to a new session?",["It uses the most specific matching policy anywhere in the list","It uses the policy with the lowest policy ID","It combines every policy that matches the session","It checks policies top-down and uses the first match"],3,"Policies are evaluated in list order and the first match wins. Policy ID is only a label; order in the list decides.","Configure firewall policies"],["fg24",0,2,"An admin adds a policy allowing HR to reach a payroll server, but HR still cannot connect. A broader deny policy for the whole LAN sits above it. What is the fix?",["Give the HR policy a lower policy ID number","Enable NAT on the HR policy","Move the HR policy above the broader deny policy","Add the payroll server to the deny policy's destination"],2,"Because the first matching policy wins, the specific allow must sit above the broad deny. Changing the ID does not change order, and NAT does not affect matching.","Configure firewall policies"],["fg25",0,2,"Denied connections are not appearing in the forward traffic log. What should the admin enable?",["Log allowed traffic set to All sessions on every accept policy","A higher event log severity level","Logging of violation traffic on the implicit deny policy","Packet capture on the WAN interface"],2,"The implicit deny policy does not log by default. Turning on its logging records the sessions it blocks; allowed-traffic logging covers accepted sessions only.","Configure firewall policies"],["fg26",0,2,"Which of these is not used when matching traffic to a firewall policy?",["The incoming and outgoing interfaces","The security profiles attached to it","The source and destination addresses","The service and schedule"],1,"FortiGate matches on interfaces, source (address or user), destination, service and schedule. Security profiles are applied after a match; they do not decide it.","Configure firewall policies"],["fg27",0,2,"Internal users need Internet access, and the FortiGate has one public IP on its WAN port. Which source NAT choice is simplest?",["Enable NAT with the outgoing interface address","Create a VIP for each internal host","Enable central SNAT with a one-to-one IP pool of public addresses","Create a port-forwarding VIP on the WAN IP"],0,"Using the outgoing interface address hides all internal hosts behind the WAN IP with port translation. VIPs are for destination NAT, and one-to-one pools need one public IP per host.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options"],["fg28",0,2,"A company owns four public IPs and wants 800 internal users to share them for outbound traffic. Which IP pool type fits?",["One-to-one","Fixed port range","Port block allocation per VIP","Overload"],3,"Overload translates many internal addresses to the pool's addresses with port translation. One-to-one gives each user a whole public IP, so only four users could be online.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options"],["fg29",0,2,"What is a limit of a one-to-one IP pool?",["It can only be used with IPsec tunnels","It randomly changes source ports on every packet it translates","It needs central SNAT to be enabled","Only as many hosts as pool addresses can connect at once"],3,"One-to-one maps each internal host to its own external address without port translation, so the pool size caps concurrent users. It works with normal policies.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options"],["fg30",0,2,"A web server at 10.0.1.10 must be reachable from the Internet on public IP 203.0.113.20. What should the admin configure?",["An overload IP pool on the WAN-to-LAN policy","A static route to 10.0.1.10 through the WAN interface","A VIP mapping 203.0.113.20 to 10.0.1.10 used in a WAN-to-LAN policy","Central SNAT with 10.0.1.10 as the translated source"],2,"Destination NAT is done with a VIP object, which is referenced as the destination of an inbound policy. IP pools and SNAT change the source, not the destination.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options"],["fg31",0,2,"External users must reach an internal app on public port 8443, but the server listens on 443. How should the VIP be set up?",["Create two VIPs, one for each port, with the same IP","Enable port forwarding and map external 8443 to mapped 443","Set the policy service to 443 and leave the VIP static","Add a custom service that rewrites 8443 to 443"],1,"Port forwarding in a VIP translates both the address and the port. Services only match traffic and never rewrite ports.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options"],["fg32",0,2,"What changes when central SNAT is enabled on a FortiGate?",["SNAT moves out of policies into a separate top-down SNAT table","Every policy automatically uses the WAN IP as source","VIPs stop working until they are recreated","Destination NAT is also moved into the individual firewall policies"],0,"With central SNAT, source NAT rules are defined in their own ordered table instead of per policy. Destination NAT still uses VIPs (DNAT and VIP table).","Configure SNAT and DNAT options"],["fg33",0,2,"Users need access to Microsoft 365, and the admin does not want to maintain its long, changing list of IPs. What should the policy use as destination?",["An Internet Service Database (ISDB) entry for Microsoft 365","An FQDN address object for www.microsoft.com and login.microsoftonline.com","A geography address object for the United States","A wildcard FQDN for *.com"],0,"ISDB objects are kept up to date by FortiGuard with the service's addresses and ports. One FQDN misses many endpoints, and geography or wildcard objects are far too broad.","Configure firewall policies"],["fg34",0,2,"Gaming sites should only be allowed from 12:00 to 13:00 on weekdays. What should be attached to the allow policy?",["A one-time schedule for today at 12:00","A web filter warning action on the gaming category","A traffic shaper limited at noon","A recurring schedule for weekdays 12:00–13:00"],3,"Recurring schedules repeat on chosen days and times. One-time schedules run once, and shapers limit bandwidth rather than time.","Configure firewall policies"],["fg35",0,2,"A policy requires users to be in the Staff group, but users never see a login page and browsing fails. DNS to the Internet is only allowed by that same policy. What is the fix?",["Change the captive portal port to TCP 53","Disable the implicit deny policy so unmatched traffic can reach the portal","Allow DNS in a policy above it with no user requirement","Set the policy action to Monitor"],2,"Before the login page can appear, the browser must resolve names, and DNS cannot trigger the login prompt. A separate DNS policy without users solves it; disabling the implicit deny would be unsafe.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication"],["fg36",0,2,"What is the difference between active and passive firewall authentication?",["Active uses RADIUS; passive uses only local users","Active prompts the user; passive identifies them from other sources","Active works only for admins; passive only for guests","Active needs FSSO; passive needs a captive portal"],1,"Active authentication asks the user for credentials (captive portal). Passive methods such as FSSO learn who is logged on from Windows logon events without a prompt.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication"],["fg37",0,2,"The FortiGate must look up users' group memberships in Active Directory over LDAP. Which bind type should the LDAP server entry use?",["Anonymous, so no credentials are stored","Regular, with a service account's DN and password","Simple, with the admin's own account","Kerberos, with no bind account configured at all"],1,"Regular bind lets FortiGate log in with a service account to search the directory. Active Directory normally rejects anonymous searches, and simple bind cannot search group memberships.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication"],["fg38",0,2,"Only members of the AD group Sales should be able to use a policy. The LDAP server is already defined. What should the admin do next?",["Create a firewall user group referencing the LDAP server and Sales DN","Create a local user named Sales with LDAP as its password","Add the LDAP server directly to the policy source","Create an administrator profile named Sales"],0,"A firewall user group with a remote server and a group match lets policies require that AD group. Policies take user groups, not LDAP server entries, and admin profiles are for administrators.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication"],["fg39",0,2,"In FSSO DC agent mode, what does the DC agent do?",["Prompts users for credentials in their browser","Sends user logons straight to FortiAnalyzer","Polls each workstation's registry remotely for the logged-on user name","Captures DC logon events and sends them to the collector agent"],3,"The DC agent runs on each domain controller and forwards logon events to the collector agent, which updates the FortiGate. Browser prompts are active authentication.","Explain how to deploy and configure FSSO"],["fg40",0,2,"The Windows team will not allow any software on the domain controllers, but the FortiGate must still identify users with FSSO. What should be used?",["DC agent mode with the agent on each member server","RADIUS single sign-on from the domain controllers","Polling mode, reading the DC security event logs remotely","Captive portal authentication with LDAP"],2,"Polling mode (from the collector agent or agentless on the FortiGate) reads logon events without software on the DCs. A captive portal is active, not single sign-on.","Explain how to deploy and configure FSSO"],["fg41",0,2,"FSSO users from most AD groups match policies, but members of the new Finance group never do. What is the most likely cause?",["Finance users log in more often than other users","The implicit deny policy is set to log all sessions","Finance is not in the groups monitored by the FSSO setup","The Finance group name contains uppercase letters that FSSO ignores"],2,"FSSO only sends groups that are selected (group filter) on the collector or FortiGate, so a new group must be added. Logging settings and name case do not stop matching.","Explain how to deploy and configure FSSO"],["fg42",0,2,"The company wants two-factor authentication for firewall users with Fortinet's own tokens. What should the admin configure?",["Enable two-factor on the admin profile only","Assign a FortiToken to each user account","Add a second LDAP server as a backup","Require longer passwords on the local users"],1,"FortiToken (hardware or mobile) is assigned per user to add a one-time code. Admin profile settings affect admins, and a backup LDAP server is redundancy, not a second factor.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication"],["fg43",0,3,"What does certificate inspection look at in an HTTPS session?",["The TLS handshake details such as SNI and certificate names","The full decrypted HTTP request and response","Only the destination port number","Files downloaded inside the session, compared with antivirus signatures"],0,"Certificate inspection reads the unencrypted handshake (SNI and certificate) without decrypting the payload, so it cannot scan files. Deep inspection decrypts the content.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates"],["fg44",0,3,"Antivirus catches test malware over HTTP but not over HTTPS. The policy uses certificate inspection. What should change?",["Switch the antivirus profile to quick scan","Enable certificate inspection on the WAN port too","Increase the antivirus maximum file size","Use deep inspection so the payload is decrypted"],3,"Without decryption the FortiGate cannot see files inside TLS. Deep inspection decrypts and re-encrypts so AV can scan them.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates"],["fg45",0,3,"After deep inspection is enabled, every user sees browser certificate warnings. What is the proper fix?",["Switch the profile back to certificate inspection only","Import each website's certificate into the FortiGate","Disable TLS 1.3 on the FortiGate","Trust the FortiGate's signing CA on all client devices"],3,"The FortiGate re-signs server certificates with its CA, so clients must trust that CA (pushed by GPO or MDM). Switching back removes decryption, and importing site certificates is not how it works.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates"],["fg46",0,3,"Legal requires that online banking traffic is never decrypted. How can deep inspection stay in place for everything else?",["Put banking sites in a static URL filter with Block","Use a separate policy with no security profiles for everyone","Exempt the Finance and Banking category in the SSL profile","Disable SSL inspection on Fridays with a schedule"],2,"SSL inspection profiles can exempt FortiGuard categories or addresses from decryption. Blocking banking is wrong, and removing profiles for everyone loses protection.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates"],["fg47",0,3,"A mobile banking app fails only when deep inspection is applied, though browsers work fine. What is the likely cause?",["The app requires certificate inspection to be off","The app pins its certificate and rejects the re-signed one","The FortiGate blocks every destination port above 1024","The app uses IPv6, which deep inspection always drops on FortiOS 7.6"],1,"Certificate pinning makes the app accept only its expected certificate, so it rejects the FortiGate's. Exempting the app's destinations from inspection fixes it.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates"],["fg48",0,3,"How does proxy-based inspection compare with flow-based inspection?",["Proxy buffers content and offers more features at higher cost","Proxy is always faster because it skips buffering","Proxy only works with IPS, not antivirus","Proxy cannot scan HTTP traffic"],0,"Proxy mode buffers whole files and supports extra features, using more resources. Flow mode inspects as packets pass for lower latency.","Identify FortiGate inspection modes"],["fg49",0,3,"In profile-based NGFW mode on FortiOS 7.6, where is the inspection mode (flow or proxy) chosen?",["In each firewall policy","Once globally for the whole FortiGate","In each antivirus profile only","On each physical interface"],0,"The inspection mode is set per firewall policy, so one policy can use proxy while another uses flow. It is not an interface or a global-only setting.","Identify FortiGate inspection modes"],["fg50",0,3,"What is different in policy-based NGFW mode?",["Firewall policies are replaced by static routes","Security profiles can only use proxy inspection","Authentication is removed from the FortiGate","Applications and URL categories go directly into security policies"],3,"In policy-based mode, security policies reference applications and categories directly, while SSL inspection and address translation are handled in separate policies. Routing and authentication still exist.","Identify FortiGate inspection modes"],["fg51",0,3,"The Social Networking category is blocked, but the marketing team must reach one company page on a blocked site. The admin adds the URL to the static URL filter. Which action lets it through?",["Allow","Monitor","Exempt","Block"],2,"The static URL filter is checked before FortiGuard categories. Exempt skips the later checks, while Allow continues on to the category check, which would still block it.","Configure web filtering"],["fg52",0,3,"A FortiGuard category is set to Warning in the web filter profile. What does a user see?",["The site with a banner that cannot be closed","A warning page that lets them choose to continue","A login page that needs credentials from an allowed group","A block page with no way to proceed"],1,"Warning shows an interstitial page and lets the user proceed. Authenticate asks for credentials, and Block gives no choice.","Configure web filtering"],["fg53",0,3,"During a FortiGuard outage every website is blocked by the web filter. Which profile setting would let sites load when a rating cannot be obtained?",["Block invalid URLs","Allow websites when a rating error occurs","Enforce safe search on all search engines","Rate images by URL"],1,"When the rating server is unreachable, this option allows sites instead of blocking them. The other options affect invalid URLs, search engines and images.","Configure web filtering"],["fg54",0,3,"What is one advantage of a DNS filter over a web filter?",["It blocks bad domains at lookup time for any app, without decryption","It decrypts HTTPS without a CA certificate","It scans downloaded files for malware","It replaces the need for firewall policies"],0,"DNS filtering rates the domain in the query, so it works for any protocol and needs no TLS inspection. It does not scan files or replace policies.","Configure web filtering"],["fg55",0,3,"The company must stop peer-to-peer file sharing such as BitTorrent. What is the most effective control?",["A firewall policy blocking TCP port 6881 only","A web filter blocking the peer-to-peer file sharing category","A DoS policy with a low session limit","An application control sensor blocking the P2P category"],3,"P2P apps change ports and use encryption, so signature-based application control is the reliable way. Blocking one port or web category misses most traffic.","Configure application control"],["fg56",0,3,"Facebook must stay allowed, but Facebook games must be blocked. How should application control be configured?",["Block the whole Social.Media category","Add facebook.com to the static URL filter as Block","Add an override that blocks only the Facebook games signature","Block the games category in the DNS filter"],2,"Application overrides act on specific signatures, so games can be blocked while Facebook stays allowed. Blocking the category or domain would block Facebook itself.","Configure application control"],["fg57",0,3,"Application control detects the cloud storage app but cannot block file uploads within it. Only certificate inspection is enabled. Why?",["Application control never works on HTTPS traffic","The app is on the FortiGuard allowlist","The actions inside the app are hidden without deep inspection","Upload control needs a proxy-mode DNS filter"],2,"The SNI reveals which app is in use, but actions such as upload are inside the encrypted payload. Deep inspection exposes them to the signatures.","Configure application control"],["fg58",0,3,"How does proxy-based antivirus handle a downloaded file?",["It scans packets as they pass and never buffers any data","It buffers the whole file, scans it, then sends it on","It only checks the file name against a list","It forwards the file and scans a copy later"],1,"Proxy mode holds the full file until the scan finishes, which lets it block or replace it. Flow mode scans as data passes and holds only the final packet until the verdict.","Configure antivirus scanning modes"],["fg59",0,3,"Office documents in email should have active content such as macros removed before delivery. Which feature does this?",["Content disarm and reconstruction in proxy-based antivirus","Flow-based antivirus with the default database","An IPS sensor with a critical-severity filter","A DNS filter blocking newly observed domains"],0,"Content disarm and reconstruction strips active content and rebuilds the file; it needs proxy-based inspection. IPS and DNS filtering do not rebuild files.","Configure antivirus scanning modes"],["fg60",0,3,"What does sending files to FortiSandbox (or FortiGate Cloud Sandbox) add to antivirus scanning?",["A faster signature database for known viruses","Decryption of HTTPS traffic without a CA certificate","Blocking of spam email by sender reputation","Behavior analysis of unknown files in a sandbox"],3,"Sandboxing runs unknown files to see what they do, catching new malware that signatures miss. It does not decrypt traffic or filter spam.","Configure antivirus scanning modes"],["fg61",0,3,"An admin wants to protect Windows servers in the DMZ from known exploits without enabling every IPS signature. What should they do?",["Enable all signatures with action pass","Apply antivirus in proxy mode to the DMZ","Create a DoS policy for the DMZ subnet","Filter the IPS sensor by server target and Windows OS"],3,"IPS sensor filters select signatures by target, OS, severity and protocol, keeping inspection relevant. Antivirus and DoS policies do not stop exploit attempts.","Configure IPS"],["fg62",0,3,"A public web server is hit by a SYN flood. Which FortiGate feature is designed to limit it before firewall policy lookup?",["An application control override for the web server","A web filter quota","A DoS policy with a tcp_syn_flood threshold","An IP pool with overload"],2,"DoS policies are checked early and apply anomaly thresholds such as SYN floods. Application control, web filtering and IP pools do not handle floods.","Configure IPS"],["fg63",0,3,"What does IPS fail-open do when enabled?",["Blocks all traffic until the IPS engine recovers","Lets traffic pass uninspected if the IPS engine is overloaded","Sends traffic to FortiSandbox when IPS fails","Stops logging to save memory"],1,"Fail-open favors availability by passing traffic when the IPS engine cannot keep up. With it disabled, traffic is dropped instead.","Configure IPS"],["fg64",0,3,"An IPS signature is blocking a legitimate internal application between two known hosts. What is the most targeted fix?",["Add an IP exemption for those hosts on that signature","Disable the whole IPS sensor on the policy","Change every signature in the sensor to monitor","Move the application's traffic to a proxy-mode policy"],0,"An IP exemption keeps the signature active for everyone else. Disabling the sensor or setting everything to monitor removes protection widely.","Configure IPS"],["fg65",0,3,"The SOC wants to stop infected hosts from contacting known command-and-control servers. Which IPS sensor option helps?",["Block outgoing connections to botnet sites","Block malicious URLs in the DNS filter only","Enable the Warning action for all categories","Set the sensor's severity filter to low"],0,"The IPS sensor's botnet C&C option uses FortiGuard's botnet database to block outgoing connections. Warning pages and severity filters do not target C&C traffic.","Configure IPS"],["fg66",0,4,"A FortiGate has two static default routes: one with distance 10 and one with distance 20. Which is used?",["Both, with traffic shared between them","The distance 20 route, as the higher value wins","Whichever was configured most recently","The distance 10 route; the other waits inactive"],3,"The lower administrative distance wins, so only the distance 10 route is in the active routing table. The other stays in the routing database and becomes active if the first is removed.","Configure static routes"],["fg67",0,4,"Two static default routes have the same distance 10, with priorities 0 and 5. What happens?",["Only the priority 5 route is installed in the table","Traffic is shared evenly between them","Both are in the table; the priority 0 route is used","Neither is installed because of a conflict"],2,"Equal distance puts both routes in the active table, and lower priority is preferred. This keeps the backup route in the table, which also helps reverse path checks.","Configure static routes"],["fg68",0,4,"Two static default routes have the same distance and the same priority. How does the FortiGate forward traffic?",["Only through the route with the lower gateway IP address","Equal-cost multipath, by default based on source IP","Round robin per packet across both","Only through the first interface in the list"],1,"Equal distance and priority create ECMP, which by default spreads sessions by source IP. Per-packet round robin is not the default and would break sessions.","Configure static routes"],["fg69",0,4,"When does a FortiGate check policy routes compared with the routing table?",["Only after a routing table lookup fails to find any match","Policy routes are checked before the routing table","Only for traffic from the FortiGate itself","Only when ECMP is enabled on the FortiGate"],1,"Policy routes are evaluated first; if one matches, it sets the path. Only when none matches does the routing table decide.","Configure static routes"],["fg70",0,4,"Traffic arriving on port3 from 172.20.5.0/24 is dropped, and debug flow shows a reverse path check failure. What is the likely cause?",["No route back to 172.20.5.0/24 through port3","The firewall policy lacks NAT","The implicit deny policy has logging on","The source subnet is on the FortiGuard blocklist"],0,"The reverse path forwarding check drops packets when the FortiGate has no route back to the source via the incoming interface. Adding a route for that subnet via port3 fixes it.","Configure static routes"],["fg71",0,4,"Which command shows inactive static routes as well as active ones?",["get router info routing-table all","get system arp","diagnose sys session list with a filter on the route","get router info routing-table database"],3,"The routing table database includes routes that are not installed, such as those with a higher distance. routing-table all shows only active routes.","Configure static routes"],["fg72",0,4,"A branch has two ISPs. The admin wants the primary default route removed if the ISP's gateway stops answering pings. What should be configured?",["A lower priority on the backup route","A blackhole route with distance 254","A link health monitor on the primary WAN","A DoS policy on the primary WAN interface"],2,"A link health monitor probes a target and removes the route when it fails, so the backup takes over. Priorities do not detect failure, and a blackhole route drops traffic.","Configure static routes"],["fg73",0,4,"Why add a blackhole route for private ranges on a site with IPsec tunnels?",["So tunnels come up faster after a reboot","So ECMP can balance across tunnels","So traffic is dropped, not sent to the Internet, if a tunnel is down","So FortiGuard updates use the tunnel"],2,"When a tunnel route disappears, traffic for private subnets would follow the default route to the ISP. A high-distance blackhole route drops it instead.","Configure static routes"],["fg74",0,4,"What does an SD-WAN performance SLA measure?",["The bandwidth used by each user and each application","Latency, jitter and packet loss to probe servers","The number of firewall policies per zone","The FortiGuard rating of each destination"],1,"Performance SLAs send probes (such as ping, HTTP or DNS) through each member and track latency, jitter and loss. SD-WAN rules use these results.","Configure SD-WAN"],["fg75",0,4,"Voice traffic must always use whichever SD-WAN member currently has the lowest jitter. Which SD-WAN rule strategy fits?",["Best quality, measuring jitter","Lowest cost (SLA)","Maximize bandwidth (SLA)","Manual, with the MPLS link first"],0,"Best quality picks the member with the best value of the chosen measure. Lowest cost picks the cheapest member that meets an SLA, not the best one.","Configure SD-WAN"],["fg76",0,4,"Traffic should use cheap broadband while it meets the SLA and only move to MPLS when broadband fails the SLA. Which strategy fits?",["Best quality","Manual","Maximize bandwidth (SLA)","Lowest cost (SLA)"],3,"Lowest cost (SLA) uses the lowest-cost member that meets the SLA target. Best quality would move traffic whenever MPLS scores even slightly better.","Configure SD-WAN"],["fg77",0,4,"After adding wan1 and wan2 to SD-WAN, the admin cannot select wan1 in a firewall policy. What should the policy reference?",["The loopback interface","A VIP for each WAN address used by the members","The HA heartbeat interface","The SD-WAN zone that contains the members"],3,"Once interfaces are SD-WAN members, policies reference the SD-WAN zone. Loopbacks, VIPs and HA ports do not replace the zone.","Configure SD-WAN"],["fg78",0,4,"SD-WAN members, SLA and rules are configured, but Internet traffic still fails. The old static default routes on wan1 and wan2 were deleted. What is missing?",["A policy route for every user subnet in the LAN","A second performance SLA","A static default route using the SD-WAN zone","An IP pool on the SD-WAN zone"],2,"SD-WAN rules only steer traffic that the routing table already sends to SD-WAN, so a route pointing at the zone is needed. Extra SLAs or IP pools do not add a route.","Configure SD-WAN"],["fg79",0,5,"Which ports must be open between two IPsec peers when one of them is behind a NAT device?",["TCP 443 and TCP 8443","UDP 500 and UDP 4500","UDP 1701 and TCP 1723","TCP 500 and TCP 4500"],1,"IKE uses UDP 500, and NAT traversal moves traffic to UDP 4500. L2TP and PPTP use 1701 and 1723.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg80",0,5,"A site-to-site tunnel's phase 1 comes up, but phase 2 never does. What is the most likely cause?",["Phase 2 selectors or proposals do not match","The pre-shared key is wrong on one of the peers","UDP 500 is blocked by the ISP","The peers use different IKE versions"],0,"Phase 1 success proves the key, IKE version and reachability are fine. Phase 2 fails when subnets (selectors) or encryption proposals differ between peers.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg81",0,5,"Why is a route-based (interface-mode) IPsec VPN usually preferred over a policy-based one?",["It creates a tunnel interface usable in routes and policies","It needs no firewall policies at all","It encrypts traffic with stronger algorithms","It can only be built with IKEv1"],0,"Route-based VPNs create a virtual interface, so routes (including dynamic routing and backup routes) and normal policies can use it. Encryption strength is the same in both.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg82",0,5,"A new route-based tunnel shows as up, but no traffic passes between the two LANs. What is most likely missing?",["A second phase 1 with aggressive mode","A VIP for each remote host","NAT-T disabled on both peers","A route via the tunnel and policies between LAN and tunnel"],3,"An up tunnel still needs a route to the remote subnet through the tunnel interface and firewall policies in both directions. VIPs and NAT-T changes are not required.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg83",0,5,"Headquarters has two ISPs and wants branch tunnels to fail over automatically. Which design fits?",["One policy-based tunnel with two phase 2 selectors","Two tunnels with identical routes and DPD turned off","Two route-based tunnels with different route distances and DPD","One tunnel whose peer is changed by hand"],2,"Two tunnels (one per ISP) with preferred and backup routes give failover, and DPD detects the dead tunnel so its route is withdrawn. Without DPD the dead tunnel may stay up.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN"],["fg84",0,5,"What does dead peer detection (DPD) do?",["Encrypts traffic twice to stop replay attacks from outside peers","Detects an unresponsive peer so the tunnel is torn down","Blocks peers found on FortiGuard blocklists","Renews the phase 2 key after every packet"],1,"DPD sends probes and brings the tunnel down when the peer stops answering, so routes can move to a backup. It does not affect encryption.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN"],["fg85",0,5,"Five sites must be connected in a full mesh of IPsec tunnels. How many tunnels are needed?",["5","10","20","25"],1,"A full mesh needs n(n−1)/2 tunnels, and 5×4/2 = 10. Hub and spoke would need only 4.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN"],["fg86",0,5,"In a hub-and-spoke VPN, spokes should build direct tunnels to each other on demand instead of sending all traffic through the hub. What should be used?",["ADVPN","A full mesh of static tunnels","Policy-based VPN on the hub","SSL VPN web mode"],0,"ADVPN lets spokes create short-cut tunnels on demand. A full static mesh works but must be built and maintained by hand.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN"],["fg87",0,5,"A branch gets a dynamic public IP from its ISP, while headquarters has a static IP. How should phase 1 be set up?",["Both use static peers with each other's current IPs","Both use dial-up peers so either side can start the tunnel","The branch uses dial-up and HQ uses static","HQ uses a dial-up (dynamic) peer; the branch points to HQ"],3,"The side with an unknown address must start the tunnel, so HQ accepts it as a dial-up peer and the branch uses HQ's static IP. Two dial-up peers could never reach each other.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg88",0,5,"Which commands show IKE negotiation messages in real time?",["get vpn ipsec stats crypto, then execute ping","diagnose sys session list, then diagnose debug reset","diagnose debug application ike -1, then diagnose debug enable","get router info routing-table all, then diagnose debug disable"],2,"The IKE debug with level -1 and debug output enabled shows negotiation steps. Session lists and routing tables do not show IKE messages.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg89",0,5,"What does enabling perfect forward secrecy (PFS) in phase 2 do?",["Uses the phase 1 key for all phase 2 keys","Sends traffic through the tunnel unencrypted as a connectivity test","Runs a new Diffie-Hellman exchange for each phase 2 key","Lets peers skip phase 1 on reconnect"],2,"PFS generates fresh key material, so a compromise of one key does not expose others. Without PFS, phase 2 keys come from phase 1 material.","Implement IPsec VPN"],["fg90",0,5,"Which command lists the current IKE (phase 1) gateways and their status?",["get system interface physical","diagnose vpn ike gateway list","diagnose sys ha status","get router info bgp summary"],1,"diagnose vpn ike gateway list shows each phase 1 gateway, its state and negotiated settings. The others show interfaces, HA and BGP.","Implement IPsec VPN"]]);
